## Problem

Prompt caching is on by default (`cache: "auto"`), but the cache TTL is fixed at the provider default — Anthropic's 5-minute ephemeral cache. For long-lived agent sessions this is the dominant cost driver:

- An orchestrator that waits on subagents or long tools for more than 5 minutes loses the entire cached prefix. The next request re-writes the whole context at the cache-write rate instead of reading it.
- On a ~440k-token context that is ~440k cache-write tokens per resume (~$2.2 on Opus at $5/M), while a cache read is ~25x cheaper per token. In one observed session ~12 cold resumptions were 51% of the session's total cost.
- Anthropic's own guidance is to set a 1-hour TTL on the prefix when an agent blocks on tool/subagent calls longer than 5 minutes, and Claude Code does exactly this (main conversation 1h, helper requests 5m).
- There is no way to express this from `opencode.jsonc`. `packages/core/src/session/model-request.ts` builds `LLM.request({ ... })` without a `cache` policy, and neither the agent nor the provider/model config schema has a cache field — even though `@opencode/ai` already supports `cache: { ttlSeconds }` (`>= 3600` maps to the Anthropic/Bedrock `1h` bucket).

## Proposal

Expose the existing cache policy at three configuration levels, resolved with **agent > model > provider** precedence:

```jsonc
{
  "providers": {
    "anthropic": {
      "cache": { "ttlSeconds": 3600 },           // default for every model of this provider
      "models": {
        "claude-opus-5-5": { "cache": "auto" }    // per-model override
      }
    }
  },
  "agents": {
    "build": { "cache": { "ttlSeconds": 3600 } }  // per-agent override
  }
}
```

The shape mirrors `@opencode-ai/llm`'s policy: `"auto" | "none" | { tools?, system?, messages?, ttlSeconds? }`. Providers that support extended retention map `ttlSeconds >= 3600` to their long-lived bucket (Anthropic/Bedrock `1h`); providers without a TTL concept keep their current behavior. A subagent can stay on the cheap 5-minute default while a long-lived primary agent opts into 1h.

## PR

Opened against `v2` and linked below.

## Summary

On `0.0.0-beta-19151`, an `ask` verdict set by a `permission.evaluate` plugin hook is **ignored**: the tool executes as if allowed and no prompt ever surfaces. A `deny` verdict from the same hook is still honored (`permission.rejected`). Any plugin implementing approval policies through this hook (ask-tier) is therefore silently bypassed on this beta, while deny-tier keeps working.

## Environment

- opencode version: `opencode2 v0.0.0-beta-19151`
- OS: macOS, Darwin 25.5.0 (darwin arm64)
- Terminal: VS Code integrated terminal (`TERM_PROGRAM=vscode`, `TERM=xterm-256color`)
- Shell: `/bin/zsh`
- Install/channel: beta
- Active plugins: one local file plugin (`~/.config/opencode/plugins/security-guard.js`, V2 object form) plus, during the investigation only, a minimal tap plugin (removed afterwards; production state restored and verified healthy via `scripts/doctor.mjs --live`)

## Reproduction

1. Install a minimal tap plugin (V2 object form) at `~/.config/opencode/plugins/zzz-eval-tap.js`:

   ```js
   import { appendFileSync } from "node:fs"
   export default {
     id: "zzz-eval-tap",
     async setup(ctx) {
       await ctx.permission.hook("evaluate", (event) => {
         try {
           const res = Array.isArray(event?.resources) ? event.resources : []
           if (res.some((r) => String(r).includes("CANARY-ASK-TEST")) && event) {
             event.effect = "ask"
             event.message = "tap canary: direct ask, no plugin logic involved"
           }
           if (res.some((r) => String(r).includes("CANARY-DENY-TEST")) && event) {
             event.effect = "deny"
             event.message = "tap canary: direct deny, no plugin logic involved"
           }
           appendFileSync("/tmp/eval-tap.log", JSON.stringify({
             action: event?.action, effect: event?.effect, n: res.length,
           }) + "\n")
         } catch {}
       })
     },
   }
   ```

2. Restart the service (`opencode2 service restart`).
3. In an agent session, run `echo CANARY-ASK-TEST`.
4. Observe: the command **executes** (`CANARY-ASK-TEST` is printed, no prompt), while `/tmp/eval-tap.log` contains `{"action":"shell","effect":"ask","n":…}` — proving the hook fired and the ask verdict was recorded, then ignored.
5. Run `echo CANARY-DENY-TEST`. Observe: the command is **blocked** with `permission.rejected` carrying the tap message — `deny` is honored.

## Expected Behavior

An `ask` verdict set by a `permission.evaluate` hook produces an approval prompt (the behavior on `0.0.0-beta-19086` for non-`cd` commands, per #47356), blocking execution until the user responds.

## Actual Behavior

The `ask` verdict is silently dropped: execution proceeds as if the effect were `allow`. Consistently reproducible across many commands and several service restarts. `deny` from the same hook works correctly, and `tool.execute.before` throws still block — the gap is specific to the `ask` effect of the permission pipeline.

Impact: every ask-tier policy implemented via `permission.evaluate` (approval prompts for ambiguous operations) silently becomes allow on this beta. Only deny-tier enforcement can be relied on.

## Additional Context

- Related: #47356 (`permission.evaluate` not firing for `cd`-led commands on beta-19086 — same channel family, one beta earlier).
- Secondary observation: on beta-19151, shell `resources` arrive **pre-segmented** — one resource per `;`-segment (`echo a; echo b` yields `n:2`; a 4-statement command yielded `n:4`). The deny canary confirms a `deny` on the whole event still blocks the full command. Plugin authors matching whole-command patterns against `resources` should be aware the unit changed from whole-command (19086) to per-segment (19151).
- Service log note: per-command `evaluated permission=…` INFO lines present on older betas no longer appear at all on 19151 (only `spawning process` lines), consistent with an evaluation-pipeline change.
- Workaround used by the affected plugin: enforce ask-tier verdicts for shell commands as hard blocks inside `tool.execute.before` (which still throws reliably), at the cost of losing the approve-and-continue workflow for legitimate ambiguous operations.
- Evidence method: tap log lines plus observed command outcomes; the tap plugin was removed and the service restarted afterwards (only the production plugin remains; `doctor --live` healthy).

## Summary

After PR #25167 (commit `560baae`, merged April 30 2026, shipped in v1.14.x), the plugin `provider.models()` hook can no longer populate models for **custom providers** — i.e. providers declared by the user in `opencode.jsonc` whose `id` is not present in the public models.dev catalog. This is a regression: the same plugin code worked on versions shipped before April 30.

## Root cause (located in `packages/opencode/src/provider/provider.ts`)

The PR moved the plugin hook iteration from running *after* config processing to *before*, and switched the lookup from the live `providers` map to the pre-config `database` map:

**Before (worked):**
```ts
// ran AFTER configProviders were merged into providers
for (const hook of plugins) {
  ...
  const provider = providers[providerID]   // live map, includes user-config providers
  if (!provider) continue
  provider.models = await models(provider, ...)
}
```

**After PR #25167 (broken for custom providers):**
```ts
// runs BEFORE configProviders loop
for (const hook of plugins) {
  ...
  const provider = database[providerID]    // models.dev catalog only
  if (!provider) continue                  // ← skips any custom provider
  provider.models = await models(provider, ...)
}
```

`database` is built from `modelsDev` (the public models.dev catalog at `~/.cache/opencode/models.json`). Any provider declared exclusively in user config — e.g. a local proxy, a self-hosted endpoint, or any other non-catalog provider — is not in `database`, so `if (!provider) continue` short-circuits the hook entirely.

The PR's stated intent (\"ensure user config takes precedence over plugin hooks\") is reasonable, but the implementation also removed the ability for plugins to register models for non-catalog providers.

## Reproduction

1. Declare a custom provider in `opencode.jsonc` not present in models.dev (e.g. an OpenAI-compatible local proxy):
   ```jsonc
   {
     \"provider\": {
       \"my-custom-proxy\": {
         \"npm\": \"@ai-sdk/openai-compatible\",
         \"name\": \"My Custom Proxy\",
         \"options\": {
           \"baseURL\": \"http://localhost:9090/v1\",
           \"apiKey\": \"unused\"
         }
       }
     }
   }
   ```

2. Write a plugin that exposes the v2 `provider.models()` hook:
   ```js
   export default {
     id: \"my-custom-proxy\",
     server: async () => ({
       provider: {
         id: \"my-custom-proxy\",
         models: async () => ({
           \"some-model\": { id: \"some-model\", providerID: \"my-custom-proxy\", api: { id: \"some-model\", url: \"http://localhost:9090/v1\", npm: \"@ai-sdk/openai-compatible\" }, name: \"Some Model\", capabilities: { /* ... */ }, cost: { input: 0, output: 0, cache: { read: 0, write: 0 } }, limit: { context: 200000, output: 64000 }, status: \"active\", options: {}, headers: {} }
         }),
       },
     }),
   };
   ```

3. **Expected**: The provider appears with the model the hook returned.
4. **Actual on v1.14.x**: The provider is registered (because the static config block exists), but the plugin hook is skipped entirely. No models from the hook appear; only models defined statically in `opencode.jsonc` show up.

Adding the provider's id to the static models.dev catalog isn't user-facing, so users with self-hosted/proxy providers cannot rely on dynamic model discovery via plugins anymore.

## Impact

This breaks any plugin that augments a non-catalog provider with dynamically discovered models — e.g. local LLM proxies that expose `/v1/models`, internal corporate gateways, OpenAI-compatible self-hosted servers. Users now have to hardcode every model in `opencode.jsonc` and update it manually whenever the upstream catalog changes.

## Suggested fix

Move the plugin hook iteration back to run *after* `configProviders` are merged into `providers`, and look up `providers[providerID]` instead of `database[providerID]`. The original ordering supported both catalog and custom providers. To preserve PR #25167's intent (user config taking precedence over plugin hooks), apply config models on top of plugin-supplied models in a final reconciliation pass, rather than by reordering the hooks.

## Environment

- OpenCode `v1.14.33`
- macOS (Apple Silicon)
- Plugin SDK `@opencode-ai/plugin@1.4.7`

### What happened?

We embed the coding-agent SDK in a long-running server ([OAR](https://github.com/botiverse/oar)): one Node process hosts many `AgentSession`s, and a session stays open for days instead of being restarted. A session's memory only grows. `SessionManager` keeps every entry in `fileEntries` / `byId` for the session's lifetime, compaction shrinks the LLM context but frees nothing, and tool-result images stay there as base64. Resuming parses the whole JSONL into memory again.

In the CLI this ends with the process. Embedded, every session adds to one heap, and one large session can take the whole host down with an out-of-memory crash. Same root cause as #6841 and #9613, which were auto-closed without review.

### Steps to reproduce

No model calls; pi 1.1.0, Node 24, heap measured after `gc()`:

1. `SessionManager.create(cwd, dir)`; append 500 tool results with 200 KB of text each and 100 with a ~1 MB base64 image.
2. `appendCompaction(...)`; `buildSessionContext()` now returns 2 messages.
3. Heap: +197 MB after appending, still +197 MB after compaction. `SessionManager.open()` on the 195 MB file adds another +195 MB.

<details><summary>Script</summary>

```js
// node --expose-gc repro.mjs
import { SessionManager } from "@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent";
import { mkdtempSync } from "node:fs";
const heap = () => (gc(), process.memoryUsage().heapUsed / 2 ** 20 | 0);
const dir = mkdtempSync("/tmp/pi-mem-"), base = heap(), sm = SessionManager.create("/tmp", dir);
const usage = { input: 1, output: 1, cacheRead: 0, cacheWrite: 0, totalTokens: 2, cost: { input: 0, output: 0, cacheRead: 0, cacheWrite: 0, total: 0 } };
sm.appendMessage({ role: "user", content: "start", timestamp: Date.now() });
let last = sm.appendMessage({ role: "assistant", content: [{ type: "text", text: "ok" }], api: "openai-completions", provider: "t", model: "t", usage, stopReason: "stop", timestamp: Date.now() });
const text = "x".repeat(200 * 1024), image = Buffer.alloc(750 * 1024, 7).toString("base64");
for (let i = 0; i < 500; i++) last = sm.appendMessage({ role: "toolResult", toolCallId: `c${i}`, toolName: "bash", content: [{ type: "text", text: text + i }], isError: false, timestamp: Date.now() });
for (let i = 0; i < 100; i++) last = sm.appendMessage({ role: "toolResult", toolCallId: `i${i}`, toolName: "shot", content: [{ type: "image", data: image + i, mimeType: "image/png" }], isError: false, timestamp: Date.now() });
console.log("appended", heap() - base, "MB");
sm.appendCompaction("summary", last, 1);
console.log("compacted", heap() - base, "MB, context", sm.buildSessionContext().messages.length);
const reopened = SessionManager.open(sm.getSessionFile(), dir);
console.log("after reopen", heap() - base, "MB, context", reopened.buildSessionContext().messages.length);
```
</details>

### Expected behavior

A session's memory bounded by what its context needs, or at least dropping after compaction, with images not kept in memory. pi-durable already works this way: entries are committed to storage, and only the latest context view stays in memory, released after `contextRetentionMs` when idle. Is that where coding-agent sessions are heading? If so we would rather wait for it than work around it. If not, would a `SessionManager` change (lazy entries, images stored outside the entries) be welcome? We can test it in a long-running embedding.

### Version

1.1.0

> AI-drafted: I asked Claude to write this up. A GPT model reproduced the bug separately, and I reviewed the text before posting.

### What happened?

After a compaction, pi-durable leaves the system baseline at the end of the model context whenever the kept recent history includes an assistant message. The next request comes out as:

```
user(summary), user, assistant, user, system
```

Requests on a conversation that was never compacted start with the system message.

`leadWithSystem` only moves the system message past preceding **user** messages ([context.ts#L176](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/3daa20280f6a7c7cbe0935680c9a38fdc3863005/packages/durable/src/harness/context.ts#L176)). The kept assistant blocks the move. On transports that keep mid-conversation system messages in place (Anthropic, openai-codex), `getInitialSystemMessage` then returns nothing. The system prompt is missing from the `system`/`instructions` field and arrives as a later system message, and the initial tool anchor for native tool changes is lost.

The 1.1.0 changelog for #10542 says "the baseline after a compaction or reset now leads the model context". The code does not do this once the kept range has an assistant message, and spec §2.1 rule 10 ([spec.md#L284](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/3daa20280f6a7c7cbe0935680c9a38fdc3863005/packages/durable/docs/spec.md#L284)) and one test ([harness-compaction.test.ts#L422](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/3daa20280f6a7c7cbe0935680c9a38fdc3863005/packages/durable/test/harness-compaction.test.ts#L422)) currently expect the trailing order. Did the #10542 fix miss this case, or is the trailing order intended?

### Steps to reproduce

In `test/harness-compaction.test.ts`, using its helpers:

```ts
it("leads with the baseline after retaining an assistant", async () => {
	const chat = await open();
	try {
		await history(chat);
		chat.faux.summaries.push(summary());
		expect((await result(chat, await chat.root.compact(undefined, context))).status).toBe("completed");
		await turn(chat, "next", "done");
		expect(chat.faux.agentRequests.at(-1)!.messages.map((m) => m.role))
			.toEqual(["system", "user", "user", "assistant", "user"]);
	} finally {
		await chat.harness.close(context);
	}
});
```

Default background threshold compaction gives the same order. A crash followed by `resume()` keeps it.

### Expected behavior

After a compaction head marker, the baseline system message leads, as it does for a fresh conversation and after a reset. A local patch does this: when the range has a head marker, it moves the first system message to the front in both `deriveRange` and `extendRange`, and the repro then puts the system message first. One risk I have not checked: a custom headed history whose first system message is a delta rather than a complete baseline.

### Version

@earendil-works/pi-durable 1.2.0 (main at 3daa202 has the same code)

### What happened?

Since there's no UI to configure OpenRouter providers, I configured `models.json` with an override:

```json
{
  "providers": {
    "openrouter": {
      "apiKey": "sk-or-v1-63139...",
      "modelOverrides": {
        "minimax/minimax-m3": {
          "name": "MiniMax M3 [rasmus@openrouter, parasail]",
          "compat": {
            "openRouterRouting": {
              "only": ["parasail/fp8"],
              "allow_fallbacks": false
            }
          }
        },
        "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash": {
          "name": "DeepSeek V4 Flash [rasmus@openrouter, baidu|gmicloud]",
          "compat": {
            "openRouterRouting": {
              "only": ["baidu/fp8", "gmicloud/fp8"],
              "allow_fallbacks": false
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

I just want to choose my providers on OpenRouter - and I want "proof" that it's working, so I'm overriding the name.

Unfortunately, this has no effect on search - so you can see:

<img width="1376" height="576" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/c055d99e-676b-4a18-9b40-a7157f54781f" />

It *does* have the name override, so at least I have proof the config is working.

However:

<img width="1376" height="708" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/147a9645-f6ee-4e33-a773-48ba4d40ac39" />

It does not seem to query the name? I can't find or keep track of my overrides.

I don't even know why any results come up at all? surely none of these have "rasmus" in the name. 😅

Could we either:

1. just make the search work for names (why doesn't it?)
  1.b. display the names on the list (why do we have names that aren't displayed?)
2. allow an `id` override, so we can change the ID that's actually visible on the list

I guess, ideally, I think the built-in providers should actually provide configuration UI? Since apparently it is very difficult to find documentation for the right keys/values to put in openrouter configuration.

I also tried adding a new openrouter provider, but neither Pi or I could find any way to make it respect the thinking option in Pi - it even plowed through the source code, and eventually concluded it couldn't make this work reliable with a custom provider.

So one other approach to consider: what if we could *extend* existing providers? Like an `extends` key for custom providers - which would make it work just like `modelOverrides`, but effectively creating a new provider in the listing rather than overriding. This would solve the search problem, and at the same time would give you easier and more reliable custom providers without needing to figure out how to replicate what Pi does internally.

It would probably fit the use case of "I just want to specify allowed providers on OpenRouter" better? Or really any scenario where you want to configure something *without* replacing the built-in options.


