## What Problem This Solves
Importing IDENTITY.md strips unmatched underscores from literal names and avatar paths: `helper_` becomes `helper` and `_portrait.png` becomes `portrait.png`.

## Fix
Preserve unmatched punctuation while unwrapping paired Markdown emphasis and code spans in the shared identity reader. Keep decorated labels and template placeholders working. Credit to @zhangguiping-xydt for the fix and regression cases. Narrow regex captures before use to fix the TS2532 errors identified in the earlier extension-package CI run.

## Evidence
Built baseline `9cc6124bcf156bb332747121d0b4de09a0ebab80` and candidate in a secretless Node 24 Docker container. Used separate isolated profiles and state roots. Imported IDENTITY.md through `openclaw --profile <profile> agents set-identity --agent main --identity-file <file> --json`, then read persisted values through `openclaw --profile <profile> config get agents.entries.main.identity --json`.

Captured readback:
```text
base literal: {"name":"helper","avatar":"portrait.png"}
candidate literal: {"name":"helper_","avatar":"_portrait.png"}
base bold: {"name":"Helper","avatar":"portrait.png"}
candidate bold: {"name":"Helper","avatar":"_portrait.png"}
base italic: {"name":"Helper","avatar":"portrait.png"}
candidate italic: {"name":"Helper","avatar":"_portrait.png"}
```
Controls used `- Name: **Helper**` and `- Name: _Helper_`. Candidate identity-file tests: 19 passed. Changed-file formatting and lint passed. No provider or channel behavior changes; no live provider gate applies.

No overlap with Pash/Sarah changes.

Simplification retained the existing shared reader and four distinct regression boundaries (plain values, decorated labels, code spans, emphasis); no additional parser owner or test scaffolding. Production +22/-2; tests +12/-0 against the merge base. Growth implements paired decoration parsing instead of destructive edge stripping.

## Merge danger
Two-way door: reverting restores previous parsing. No schema migration or new configuration. Persisted literal identity values remain ordinary strings. Blast radius is identity-file imports and identity presentation; channel delivery and provider behavior are unchanged.

## What Problem This Solves

`beginForegroundSessionMaintenance` (`src/agents/session-maintenance/coordinator.ts`) reserves foreground priority for a session key, asks any optional (preemptible) maintenance owners to yield, and then awaits the existing owners' `done` before handing back its `release` closure.

It accepted no caller signal, so that wait could not be cancelled:

1. **A stopped caller stayed blocked.** Once the reservation was taken, the caller was pinned to that await until an unrelated owner's cleanup finished. A Stop landing in that window could not settle the run — it had to wait out someone else's cleanup.
2. **An already-stopped caller still preempted.** With no up-front `aborted` check, a caller whose signal had already fired still reserved the slot and aborted in-flight optional maintenance, for work it was never going to do.

Two admission sites reach this from a real user turn, in this order:

- `src/auto-reply/reply/reply-turn-admission.ts` — `admitReplyTurn` reserves foreground for every `visible` turn. This is the barrier a Gateway `chat.send` hits **first**.
- `src/agents/embedded-agent-runner/run/lane-controller.ts` — reserves again before enqueueing the foreground turn, behind the first.

## Changes

- `beginForegroundSessionMaintenance` takes an optional `abortSignal` and rejects a pre-aborted caller before reserving or preempting anything.
- It keeps current `main`'s wait on **every** independent maintenance owner (`Promise.all(existing.map((owner) => owner.done))`); only that wait is raced against the caller signal.
- The wait on existing owners is raced against that signal using the existing `racePromiseWithAbortSignal` helper. On rejection it releases **only its own** reservation and rethrows; maintenance writer completion, `releaseWrites()`, predecessor exclusion, and other readers' fences are untouched.
- `admitReplyTurn` reserves through `reserveForegroundUnlessStopped`, a thin coordinator wrapper that forwards its `upstreamAbortSignal` and turns a Stop during the wait into "no reservation" (only when the rejection is an `AbortError` **and** the caller's signal has aborted). The admission loop's existing first check then returns the existing `{ status: "skipped", reason: "aborted" }` outcome. The wrapper lives in the coordinator because current `main` leaves `reply-turn-admission.ts` at its 700-line `max-lines` budget.
- `lane-controller.ts` forwards its composed `abortSignal` and rechecks cancellation before queue entry, so a cancelled caller surfaces the lane's own abort error — preserving `Error` identity and string-reason `AbortError` causes.
- In both callers only `AbortError` rejections are re-mapped; a genuine maintenance failure is rethrown untouched even if the signal has aborted by then.

Scope is foreground reservation admission only.

## Evidence

### Real runtime proof (Gateway transport)

`test/pr148895-real-runtime-proof.e2e.test.ts` drives a real Gateway over its WebSocket RPC transport against a loopback Anthropic-format provider — no mocked transport, no direct handler calls, no credentials. Per case it: runs a warm-up turn that genuinely completes, holds a real maintenance owner's tracked cleanup, sends a foreground `chat.send` on the same canonical session key, waits until the foreground reservation exists, then Stops it with `chat.abort`. It runs once with a non-preemptible owner and once with a preemptible one.

The fixture supplies only the maintenance workload and its release gate. The coordinator, admission path, gateway, and network client are the real ones; reservation counts are read from the live coordinator singleton and never written by the harness.

