## Summary
Workspace hooks are documented as off until the operator sets `hooks.internal.entries.<hookKey>.enabled: true` for them. A hook under `<workspace>/hooks/` can skip that opt-in by declaring `metadata.openclaw.hookKey` equal to the key of a bundled, plugin, or managed hook. With config that enables only the bundled `session-memory` hook, a workspace hook with `"hookKey": "session-memory"` is reported as enabled and its handler is imported into the Gateway process, even though the operator never enabled it.

This change makes hook selection stricter: a workspace hook can no longer take another source's config key, the same way it already cannot take another source's hook name.

## Root cause
`resolveHookEntries` in `src/hooks/policy.ts` drops a workspace hook only when its **name** matches a higher-trust hook. Enablement is then looked up by the resolved `hookKey` (`resolveHookEnableState` and `shouldIncludeHook` use `resolveHookConfig(config, hookKey)`, and `isHookNameSelected` matches either name or `hookKey`). Since `hookKey` comes from the hook's own frontmatter, a workspace hook with a different name and a borrowed key gets past the name check and reads the other hook's entry.

```ts
// before
  return Array.from(merged.values());
}
```

## Fix
After the existing name-based merge, collect the names and resolved keys of every non-workspace hook. Drop any workspace hook whose resolved `hookKey` is in that set, and report it through the existing `onCollisionIgnored` callback so the current `Ignoring ... because it cannot override ... hook code` warning appears.

```ts
// after
  const resolved = Array.from(merged.values());
  const reservedKeys = new Map<string, T>();
  for (const entry of resolved) {
    if (entry.hook.source !== "openclaw-workspace") {
      reservedKeys.set(entry.hook.name, entry);
      reservedKeys.set(resolveHookKey(entry.hook.name, entry), entry);
    }
  }
  return resolved.filter((entry) => {
    if (entry.hook.source !== "openclaw-workspace") {
      return true;
    }
    const kept = reservedKeys.get(resolveHookKey(entry.hook.name, entry));
    if (!kept) {
      return true;
    }
    opts?.onCollisionIgnored?.({ name: entry.hook.name, kept, ignored: entry });
    return false;
  });
}
```

## Why this is the right boundary
`resolveHookEntries` is where source precedence is already enforced, and it already says workspace hook code cannot override other sources. All discovery consumers go through it: the Gateway loader (`prepareInternalHooks` via `prepareWorkspaceHookEntries`), `buildWorkspaceHookStatus` (`openclaw hooks list/info/check`, Gateway `hooks.status`), the CLI enable/disable path, and the bootstrap-files diagnostics (`loadWorkspaceHookEntries`). So one check covers selection, enablement, status, and loading together, and no caller needs its own guard.

What stays the same:
- Workspace hooks with their own keys work as before, including keys shared with other workspace hooks.
- Managed hooks can still override bundled and plugin hooks.
- Bundled, plugin, and managed hooks can still share keys with each other. Only the lowest-trust source is restricted.

The `hookKey` row in `docs/automation/hooks/writing-hooks.md` now describes this.

