## Summary

On macOS, a Gateway service environment value that contains a newline (for example a PEM private key or other multi-line secret coming from `~/.openclaw/.env` or config env vars) is written correctly at install time, but the next `openclaw gateway restart` silently corrupts it. The restart path rereads the generated LaunchAgent env file, keeps only the first physical line of the value with a stray leading `'`, and writes that truncated value back. After the restart the Gateway receives `'-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----` instead of the full key. The same lenient reader backs the service `readCommand` used by install and repair flows to preserve existing environment, so a reinstall can carry the corrupted value forward too.

## Root cause

`src/daemon/launchd-plist.ts`, `readLaunchAgentEnvironmentFile`. The writer (`quoteLaunchAgentEnvironmentValue`) emits a single-quoted shell literal that spans physical lines when the value contains a newline, and only the strict (`requireEffective`) branch knew how to join those lines back:

```ts
// before
const line = options?.requireEffective ? rawLine.trimStart() : rawLine.trim();
...
let parsedValue = parseGeneratedEnvValue(value);
if (options?.requireEffective) {
  // The writer's quoted literals can span physical lines; retain their exact newline bytes.
  while (
    quoteLaunchAgentEnvironmentValue(parsedValue) !== value.trim() &&
    index + 1 < lines.length
  ) {
    value += `\n${lines[++index]}`;
    parsedValue = parseGeneratedEnvValue(value);
  }
  ...
}
environment[key] = parsedValue;
```

Without `requireEffective`, `export KEY='line1` is parsed on its own: `parseGeneratedEnvValue` sees an opening quote with no closing quote and returns `'line1` verbatim, and the remaining lines of the literal are skipped as unrecognized syntax. `rewriteLaunchAgentPlistForRestart` reads with these lenient options and republishes the env file from the result, so the corruption becomes durable.

## Fix

Join continuation lines in both modes, keyed on whether the shell literal is still inside an open single quote, and keep the strict writer-syntax check only for `requireEffective`:

```ts
// after
const line = rawLine.trimStart();
...
// The writer's quoted literals can span physical lines; retain their exact newline bytes.
while (hasOpenSingleQuote(value) && index + 1 < lines.length) {
  value += `\n${lines[++index]}`;
}
const parsedValue = parseGeneratedEnvValue(value);
// Strict inspection accepts the writer's literal syntax, never shell expressions.
if (
  options?.requireEffective &&
  quoteLaunchAgentEnvironmentValue(parsedValue) !== value.trim()
) {
  throw new Error("Unsupported LaunchAgent environment value");
}
```

`hasOpenSingleQuote` scans with POSIX `sh` rules (a backslash outside quotes escapes the next byte, `'` toggles quoting), so the writer's `'\''` escape stays balanced and the scan closes exactly at the end of the writer's literal. Using that open-quote check (not "does not match the writer form yet") matters for the lenient path: a hand-edited unquoted line such as `export A=$(id)` is not open and so does not swallow the following lines. The first line now uses `trimStart` in both modes so whitespace that sits inside the quoted literal at the end of its first line is kept. `parseGeneratedEnvValue` still trims around the literal, so single-line values parse the same as before.

## Why this is the right boundary

`readLaunchAgentEnvironmentFile` is the single reader for the generated LaunchAgent env file. Fixing it covers every caller: `rewriteLaunchAgentPlistForRestart` (`openclaw gateway restart` and bootstrap repair), `readLaunchAgentProgramArguments` (service `readCommand` used by install/repair to preserve existing env, status and doctor), and `launchd-update-jobs`. Strict inspection behaves the same as before: valid writer output is accepted, and shell expressions are still rejected with the same error. The systemd and Scheduled Task writers reject CR/LF in env values at write time (`assertNoSystemdLineBreaks`, `assertNoCmdLineBreak`), so only the macOS path can store a multi-line value and need this read-back fix. No change to update behavior: the env file format and writer are unchanged, and this only affects how an existing generated file is read back.

## Verification

- Added a regression case to `src/daemon/launchd-plist.test.ts` that reads a generated env file holding a multi-line value (with trailing spaces on its first line), a value containing an escaped apostrophe, and a following entry, through the lenient (non-`requireEffective`) options. It fails on main (`KEY` comes back as `'-----BEGIN KEY-----`) and passes with this patch.
- I did not run the local gates (vitest, oxlint, oxfmt, tsgo) on this machine. CI covers them.
- I also checked the same input against the strict reader and against a shell-expression line, on main and on the patch: strict output is the same, `export A=$(id)` is still rejected under `requireEffective`, and the lenient reader still returns it literally without consuming the next line.

## Real behavior proof

